## 1. Introduction
With the depletion of fossil energy and the growing population, exploring new sustainable energy with low environmental impact has been considered as an extremely urgent and important task [1–5]. Free energy such as solar, wind, tidal and hydroelectricity are renewable, green and safe; whereas, these energies are changeable and unreliable because they are great dependence on the terrain and weather condi­ tions. Also, these energy sources are intermittent and difficult to be gathered or released as needed, especially for solar, wind and hydro­ electricity [6–8]. Utilizing energy-conversation technologies are essen­ tial for the development of energy resource. Electrocatalysis is considered as one of the most promising approaches to relieve the contemporary seriously energetic dilemma. The electrocatalytic pro­ cesses refer several energy devices like fuel cell, metal-air batteries and water splitting [9–11]. Fundamentally, these technologies or devices involve different energy-conversion reactions, including the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), which produce energy via directly converting chemical energy into electrical energy, or reserve energy vice versa [12,13].
Up to now, noble-metal-based materials show excellent performance for various kinds of electrochemical reactions. As is well-known, these precious metals are expensive and scarce, which does not meet the criteria for large-scale commercialization [14–16]. Besides, when exposed to a series of chemical compounds (e.g., $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , CO, etc.), noble metal catalysts may give rise to the issues of poisoning, resulting in a greatly loss of activity [17,18]. To overcome these problems, seeking novel electrocatalysts to replace precious-metal-based materials has received increasing attention. Over the past decades, polymetallic cat­ alysts have attracted world-wide research interests due to their strong cooperation effects between different components. In principle, designing an ideal electrocatalyst need to consider many fundamental features like catalytic activity, electrochemical conductivity, durability, robustness and cost. However, using traditional metal salts as precursors is difficult to control the composition, surface area, and porosity. Fortunately, the discovery of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) pro­ vides a brilliant opportunity to facilitate the multi-metal catalyst syn­ thesis with desirable properties.
MOFs, or called porous coordination polymers (PCPs), comprising of metal ions/clusters as “nodes” joined together with organic ligands/ linkers as “bridges”, are a kind of highly crystalline porous materials with periodic structural units. Until now, more than 70,000 MOFs have been evolved [19], while the narrow choices of MOFs caused by their instability in water and weak conductivity restrict their further appli­ cation [20–22]. Along with the phenomenal successes of MOF-related materials, MOF-derived nanomaterials have sprung up in light of their inherited morphology, ultrahigh pore volume and surface area as well as high tunability in textural characteristics [23–25]. Their promising po­ tential has been manifested in the areas of gas sorption and storage, energy transfer, chemical catalysis and environmental remediation [26–31]. Particularly, electrocatalysis is becoming one of the most promising applications for MOF derivatives [32–36]. Utilizing MOFs as templates/precursors is an attracting route to fabricate polymetallic electrocatalysts. The precise atoms arrangement of MOF precursors enables the multi-metal catalysts with uniform distribution of the metal species, leading to powerful interaction between diverse metals [37,38].
It is worth pointing out that several excellent reviews about MOF derivatives in electrocatalysis have been published, but they mainly focus on MOF-derived carbonaceous materials [39–41]. Multi-metallic nanomaterials as promising functional materials have shown great po­ tential in many fields as a consequence of their unique physicochemical properties. The strong synergistic effects between metal species in multimetallic nanomaterials give rise to enhanced performance than mono­ metallic counterparts. On the other hand, the large diversity of metal combinations offers both design flexibility and potential for de­ velopments, significantly expanding the toolbox for the construction of desirable structured nanomaterials. Although the rapid development in this field, the exploitation of MOF-derived metal-based components, particularly multi-metal nanostructured materials is still inadequate. A timely review with emphasis on the general and effective strategies to­ wards design, synthesis and utilization of MOF derivatives has farreaching significance. In this review, the design strategies involved in MOF-derived multi-metal nanomaterials are first summarized and broadly falls into six categories, including metal nodal substitution, seed epitaxial growth, ion-exchange strategy, guest species encapsulation, solution impregnation and combination with extraneous substrates. Afterwards, the electrocatalytic applications of these materials as elec­ trocatalysts in OER, ORR and HER are discussed. To close, some chal­ lenges and personal insights related to the fabrication and applications of these functional materials are proposed. This review is expected to trigger strong inspiration and provides research opportunities for further development of improved MOF-derived multi-metal nanomaterials in the field of electrochemical energy-conversion, and extended applica­ tions in electrochemistry, coordination chemistry and environmental protection.
## 2. MOFs as precursors for multi-metal nanomaterials
The derivation of MOFs emerged as a class of fascinating nanoporous materials are deeply appealing for the current research interests due to their exceptional features. The metal center in MOFs are typically transitional and lanthanide metal ions (e.g., $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , etc.) and the linkers are generally organic oxygen or nitrogen-rich components (e.g., azoles, imidazoles, pyrazolates, carboxylates, etc.) [42–45]. In general, MOFs can be converted into metallic components and carbonaceous materials simultaneously, and the formed metal nanoparticles are uniformly distributed in carbon skeletons, which has been regarded as a grand challenge using other methods. It deserves to be pointed out that the successful fabrication for MOF-derived multimetal nanomaterials highly depends on the bond breaking of the parent MOF structures. The thermal treatment and solvothermal method are usually used for preparing various MOF-derived metal-based materials [46–48]. From the perspective of synthesis, though traditional prepa­ ration methods using co-precipitation, sol-gel, combustion and impregnation are implemented economically on commercial scales [49,50], some shortcomings still remain visible in most cases, including non-uniform distribution of metal species, lacking in pores, sintering and decrease of surface area [51–55]. For example, $\mathrm{CuFe_{2}O_{4M O F}}$ catalyst prepared by MOF-templated method possessed higher BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface area, pore volume and crystallinity than those synthesized using hydrothermal process and sol-gel combustion method, leading to more exposure active sites and stable activity [56]. Similarly, the Co/Fe Mate´rial Institut Lavoisier (MIL)-derived $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}}$ catalyst showed better catalytic activity than that obtained from con­ ventional one-step hydrothermal method [57]. Developing MOFs as platform for preparation of polymetallic electrocatalysts is favorable. Since the electrocatalytic performance closely correlates with material physicochemical properties, synthesizing target electrocatalyst with specific properties through tailoring compositions, morphologies, and structures is of great concern [58].
Using MOFs as precursors/templates to synthesize multi-metallic electrocatalysts have many advantages (Fig. 1): (i) the well-dispersed active species owing to the intrinsic topology of MOFs even with the integration of different metal ions; (ii) the feasible introduction of het­ eroatoms with different electronegativity (such as N, S, P, B, etc.) to modify the electronic structure; (iii) precise regulation of metal pro­ portion and micro-morphology by controlling the reactant ratio and themolysis conditions (e.g., heating rate, pyrolysis temperature and time); (vi) the preserved porous structure and high surface area of pristine MOFs endowing abundant cavities and channels; (v) prevention of metal migration/aggregation due to the confined effect of MOF skeleton [59–63]. Based on above considerations, selecting MOF as platform for fabrication of efficient electrocatalyst is an attracting approach.
Indeed, MOF-derived monometallic compounds possess meagre ac­ tivity in certain reactions, and thus developing polymetallic catalysts seems to be an exciting way to achieve a great improvement in perfor­ mance (Fig. 2) [64,65]. Multi-metal systems have been used to optimize the adsorption energy, electrical conductivity and stability of catalysts, or to reduce the usage of precious metal maintaining the catalytic ac­ tivity. The secondary metal introduced into the MOF crystal lattices could markedly facilitate charge transfer between diverse ions as well as modulate the electronic structure and surface atomic coordination [66–69]. Based on the theoretical studies, foreign metals have two pri­ mary effects on the electronic structure: the ligand effect and the strain effect [70–72]. The former refers to the perturbations of the electronic structure induced by charge transfer between neighboring metals. The later originates from variations in the lattice parameters on the surface. The introduction of secondary metal would tune the lattice parameters and thus the overlap degree between atomic orbitals, further affecting the electronic structure. Both effects are generally inseparable and collectively lead to the modified electronic structure. On the other hand, the secondary metal ions could serve as a geometrical spacer to spatially insulate other metal species [73,74]. Taking a rod-like NiS/ZnOS as an example [75], the Zn species existed in parent MOFs serve as geomet­ rical spacer between Ni to restrain its sintering, leading to the formation of smaller NiS and thus benefiting the creation of abundant active sites as well as the decrease of activation energy for hydrogen reduction. In another case, the introduction of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ in MgNi-MOF-74 helped to realize spatial separation of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in M-oxo chains, resulting in the for­ mation of single-atom Ni. With these intriguing merits in mind, multimetal electrocatalysts starting from MOFs has aroused intense atten­ tion worldwide. Fig. 3 summarized the development milestones of MOFs and MOF-based polymetallic nanomaterials used for electrocatalytic applications.
The good strategy and fabrication of multi-metallic nanomaterials derived from MOFs is important for a better understanding of the structure-property relationship. The most crucial factor in the activity of these nanomaterials depends on morphology that includes shape, size, arrangement and structure. Particularly, the nanostructures materials with large surface area, numerous active sites, hierarchical pores, uni­ form doping and high conductivity are beneficial to achieve superior electrocatalytic performance. Through self-templating approach, the derivative’s morphology is mainly inherited from the parent MOF owing to the sufficient thermal stability. Nevertheless, for the synthesis by an external-templating method, various morphologies could be obtained by calcining the combination of MOFs and templates. Previous studies re­ ported that the reaction conditions such as temperature, organic solvent and metal content also significantly affect the sample morphology [76–78].
In general, these nanomaterials in terms of morphology could be classified as: 0D (polyhedrons, hollow and core-shell structures), 1D (nanotubes and nanorods), 2D (nanoflakes, nanoplates and nanofilms), and 3D (foams, flowers and honeycomb like frameworks). Polyhedrons including dodecahedrons, hexahedrons and octahedrons are the most prominent and well-studied structures. Typically, ZIF precursors that have many edges and corners are frequently utilized to produce this type of nanostructures with numerous accessible active sites [79]. Hollow nanomaterials are characteristic of large surface areas, high loading capacities and low densities compared to their solid counterparts. Coreshell structures are able to provide good dispensability, functionality and stability [80]. Mostly, pre-designed core-shell MOF precursors are applied for the preparation of these nanostructures. By seed epitaxial growth method, hollow/core-shell structure can be easily obtained. 1D nanostructures with certain features same as 0D and 2D materials have different properties from bulk materials, such as mechanical, electronic and optical property [81,82]. For the synthesis of 1D nanomaterials via self-templating method, there are few 1D MOF precursors are available. The large surface area of 2D nanomaterials enable them possess more exposed active centers, as compared to bulk materials. MOF precursors with lamellar morphology are usually used for the fabrication of 2D nanostructures via self-templating approach [83,84]. By combining MOFs with flexible substrates, 3D nanomaterials with versatile mor­ phologies can be achieved, such as foams, sponges and flowers like frameworks. Compared to lower-dimension nanomaterials, 3D nano­ structures with interconnected networks have larger accessible surface area. Benefiting from the spatial arrangement of particles in 3D frame­ work, nanomaterials acquire the tunable host-guest interactions, sizeselective properties and surface functionalities, leading to an enhance­ ment of overall electrochemical activity and stability [85,86].
## 3. Design of multi-metallic nanomaterials from MOFs
As is well-known, MOFs are constructed through organic ligands coordinated with metal nodes. Their highly flexibility in topological structure and composition make them as suitable platform to fabricate multi-metal nanomaterials with excellent performance. In this section, six primary strategies in reference to the construction of multi-metallic nanomaterials from MOFs are systematically discussed for the first time, including metal nodal substitution, seed epitaxial growth, ionexchange strategy, guest species encapsulation, solution impregnation and combination with extraneous substrates (Fig. 4). In some cases, these methods are utilized simultaneously. The advantages and disad­ vantages of these strategies have been summarized in Table 1.
## 3.1. Metal nodal substitution strategy
Metal nodal substitution refers to incorporation of the foreign metal species with similar ionic radii of the metals into host framework crystal lattice [87,88]. This popular strategy is regarded as the most straight­ forward method for the preparation of mixed metal nanomaterials due to its prominent features. Specifically, the facile substitution of metal nodes using one-pot reaction, i.e., different metal ions and linkers are simultaneously dissolved in organic solvent or water and then followed by self-assembly process to obtain target products, can retain the orig­ inal MOF crystal structure without obvious destroy. In this process, the metal ions come from both soluble salts and metal foils (e.g., copper foils).
The usage of different metal salts in MOF synthesis shows great impact on the particle sizes ranging from the nanoscale to microscale. In the work of Torad, nano-sized zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) crystals with an average size of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ were prepared when $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ was utilized as zinc source [89]. Owing to the weak solvation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions in methanol solution, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions could coordinate with 2-meth­ ylimidazole (2-MIM) easily, thus leading to the rapid generation speed of ZIF-8 with small size. While the particle size was increased to $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ when $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ replaced with $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ . This could be put down to the strong interaction between the dissolved $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions and $\left(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O}\right)^{-}$ ions along with the solvation of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ions, which drastically slowed down the nucleation.
It is noteworthy that some specific agents such as poly(vinyl­ pyrrolidone) (PVP), cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and so­ dium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) are usually employed for the surface modification in the synthesis process [90–92]. Nanoparticles with various size, shape and composition can be fully confined and well dispersed inside MOF matrix with the help of surfactant [93–95]. In Pan’s work, ZIF-8 showed a typical rhombic dodecahedron with the mean particle size of $4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ without CTAB addition [96]. When the CTAB contents in the MOF synthesis solution increased from $0.0025~\mathrm{wt\%}$ to $0.025\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the morphology of ZIF-8 gradually changed from truncated rhombic dodecahedron to truncated cubes and the size continually decreased from $2.8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to $110\mathrm{nm}$ , in which the long hydrocarbon chains play a vital role in changing the shape and the MOFs size. Similar phe­ nomena also were observed by using cetyltrimethylammonium chloride (CTAC) and trimethylstearylammonium chloride (STAC). Zhan and coworkers reported the surfactant-induced amorphization of ZIF-90 (Fig. 5a) with a random network topology instead of the sodalite to­ pology (Fig. 5b) [97]. Monocrystalline ZIF-90 with rhombic dodecahe­ dral shape can be obtained by mixing zinc salts and imidazole-2- carboxaldehyde directly. After the addition of CTAC surfactant in the synthetic solution, $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{m}}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}90$ with hierarchical dandelion-like morphology composed of many radially distributed nanorods as its building blocks was produced (Fig. 5c, d). Aside from the CTAC, other surfactants such as anionic surfactants (SDS) and cationic surfactants (CTAB, and dodecyltrimethylammonium bromide, DTAB) were also studied in their work. $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{m}}\mathsf{Z I F-}90$ with similar rodlike building units and their assemblies was yielded by both cationic surfactants (Fig. 5e, f). Using DTAB could obtain the pure $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{m}}\mathsf{Z I F-}90$ , while a small number of crystalline ZIF-90 was also generated when CTAB was utilized (Fig. 5g).
Copper foil recently is exploited for synthesis of polymetallic ZIF precursors containing $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ by a simple redox reaction (i.e., $2\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{Cu}$ $=\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})$ . The adoption of copper foil to obtain mixed metal MOFs are not only able to introduce the Cu species with high activity, but also prevent the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ from oxidation during the growth of MOFs.
Generally, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ is more favorable to achieve uniform Fe doping in ZIF-8 scaffolds compared with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ because of the severe structural distortion with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ substitution by $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ [98]. Whereas, Fe doped ZIFs should be synthesized under an oxygen-free environment to avoid the trans­ formation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , which makes the operation process more complicated and cumbersome [98,99].
Using copper foil as Cu source and reductant, Wang and co-workers prepared Fe, Cu-coordinated ZIF-derived Cu@Fe-N-C by thermal treat­ ment in Ar gas (Fig. 6a) [100]. The color change of solution from yellow to light green verified the successful synthesis. The introduced Fe and Cu ions in zinc-based MOFs gave rise to a morphological variation and great increase of MOF size due to partial deprotonation of organic ligands and extended growth directions in the existence of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ . The ho­ mogenous distribution of Cu and Fe species throughout the entire ar­ chitecture was clearly discerned from the elemental mapping (Fig. 6b). Through the same reaction process, Zhang et al. developed multi-metal (Fe, Cu, Zn, and Co) mixed ZIF by a one-pot procedure of an in situ ionic reaction in the ambient atmosphere (Fig. 6c) [101]. Copper foil was immersed into the solution having $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions to yield ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ . After calcination, an exceptionally porous carbon poly­ hedron $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{FC-C@NC})$ grafted with plentiful carbon nanotubes (CNTs) were synthesized (Fig. 6d). The ultrafine Co NPs with lattice sites partly substituted by Cu and Fe were embedded into the resultant $\operatorname{FC-C@NC}$ (Fig. 6e). The obtained $\operatorname{FC-C}_{\mathcal{\Theta}\mathrm{NC}}$ nanomaterials were characteristic by large surface area, highly porous structure, active metal sites and conductive carbon frameworks, contributing to outstanding ORR ac­ tivity. Without the addition of copper foil, Fe-CoZn-ZIF was failed to directly prepare at the same conditions by a co-precipitation method in the solution containing $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Fig. 6f). These works pave a way for efficient and convenient design of Cu-assisted generation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ during the MOF-growth.
Using metal nodal substitution strategy through a one-pot process is believed to be a facile and usual route for preparation of mixed metal nanomaterials. Various metal salts coordinated with organic linkers in a homogeneous solution, in which surfactants also can be used to func­ tionalize MOFs, making it easy to control the particle size, morphology and chemical composition. The coordination environment provided by MOF ligands helps to adjust the dispersion of metal active sites by the selective change of MOF nodes and the incorporation of other metals. Moreover, the doped metals can be selectively removed. The original structure of MOFs can be preserved well owing to the lattice substitution of metal dopant. On the other hand, the simple but effective modulation of metal content in MOF scaffold also can be realized by changing the ratio of metal salt precursors. Whereas, those metal ions with large differences in electronic configuration are still difficult to be doped into the metal host frameworks, stemming from their difference in coordi­ nation behavior with organic ligands.
## 3.2. Seed epitaxial growth strategy
Seed epitaxial growth is an efficient method to fabricate dual-MOF core-shell structures [102–105]. Owing to their similar crystal struc­ ture and lattice constant, the MOF shell epitaxially grown on another MOF seed can be successfully achieved. Using such strategy, the advantages of MOFs can be incorporated fully to generate significant synergistic effects [106]. Early in 2014, Huang et al. reported core-shell $\mathrm{Co_{3}[F e(C N)_{6}]_{2}\ @N i_{3}[C o(C N)_{6}]_{2}}$ nanocubes using PBA as starting mate­ rials [107]. Specifically, NiCo PBA as seed was fabricated by coprecipitation method in the aqueous mixture of $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}[{\mathrm{Co}}$ $(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ . In light of the similar crystal structure and unit cell parameter, a layer of CoFe PBA was epitaxially deposited onto the surface of NiCo PBA nanocubes to form a core-shell CoFe@NiCo precursor. During MOF epitaxial growth, sodium citrate as an additive interacted with the metal ions in the homogeneous solution to decrease the coordination rate between the ligands and metal ions. Later on, the same group proposed another study by using core-shell $\mathrm{Fe_{2}N i}$ MIL-88/Fe MIL-88 as templates [108]. The dual-MILs nanorods were successfully fabricated through consecutive hydrothermal reaction of Fe MIL-88 precursors in the so­ lution containing initially formed $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ MIL-88 seeds. Then, core-shell MILs were converted into the final $\mathrm{NiFe_{2}O_{4}/F e_{2}O_{3}}$ nanotubes by a post-annealing process. Huang et al. developed a facile method to fabricate $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}/C u O}$ (sub)microcages using dual-MOF $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -Co PBAs@HKUST-1 heterostructures as both the precursor and selfsacrificing template [109]. $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -Co PBAs were first prepared and then used as seeds to synthetic core-shell $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ -Co PBAs $@$ HKUST-1 crystals. After thermal annealing in air, $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -Co PBAs $@$ HKUST-1 composites were chemically transformed into $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}/C u O}$ (sub)microcages, which effectively retained the morphology and porous structure of original MOFs, synchronously achieving homogeneous heteroatom doping and unique hollow architecture.
In Aslam’s work, time dependent ZIF-67@ZIF-8/ZIF-67 nano­ particles were prepared by the kinetically controlled synthesis and then converted to hollow $\mathrm{ZnCoS@CogS_{8}/N C}$ (Fig. 7a) after sulfidation and calcination [110]. Cobalt precursors were added into the solution of 2- MIM ligands and the homogeneous solution transformed from trans­ parent to turbid over time. After 7 min, Zn salts were dissolved in the above mixed solution to afford the bi-metallic Co-to-Zn nanoparticles. The edge growth of inside ZIF-67 crystal and outside ZIF-8/ZIF-67 structure was clearly confirmed by field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM) (Fig. 7b-c). Through sulfidation and pyrolysis pro­ cess, ZIF-67 $@$ ZIF-8/ZIF-67 was evolved to yolk-shelled $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnCoS}}@{\mathrm{CoS}}$ and further converted to single-holed hollow $\mathrm{ZnCoS@Co_{9}S_{8}/N_{\ell}}$ -doped car­ bon with core-shell structure. The N-doped porous carbon endowed the catalyst with fast electron transport to achieve exceptionally high-rate capability. Huang et al. fabricated $\mathbf{MoC_{2}/C o}$ embedded hybrid hollow N-doped carbon polyhedrons $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{CoMo}@\mathbf{HNCP})$ using core-shell ZIF$8@\mathrm{HZIF}$ -CoMo as precursors (Fig. 7d-h) [111]. In the presence of PVP, the as-prepared ZIF-8 microcrystals as seeds reacted with Co ions, $\mathrm{H_{2}M o O_{4}}$ and organic ligands to form polymetallic core-shell architec­ ture. By further annealing under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere, the target $\operatorname{CoMo@HNCP}$ were obtained. Benefiting from the homogeneous distri­ bution of metallic Co and $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ NPs in the hollow N-doped carbon shell, the produced multiple heterogeneous interfaces helped to improved performance, making it superior to that of ZIF-8 derived N-doped carbon and HZIF-CoMo derived bimetallic carbon polyhedron. Undesirably, the reaction of HZIF-CoMo shell growth on ZIF-8 core are time-consuming and power-wasting, which limited this method for further utilization and large-scale production in industry.
The polyhedral core-shelled MOF architecture can be facilely fabri­ cated by using seed epitaxial growth strategy. After heat treatment, the core-shell/hollow nanomaterials are usually obtained, and the integrity of parent MOF is able to retain to a great extent. Compared to the single MOF crystal, the combination of different types of MOF can exert complementary and synergetic effect effectively. Typically, the ZIF-67 has been well demonstrated to afford its derivate with uniform N doping, $\mathsf{C o N}_{\mathbf{x}}$ sites and high graphitization degree yet low surface area; the ZIF-8, isostructural to ZIF-67, should be a suitable candidate because it can offer derivate with high surface area and porosity. Thus, the bimetallic ZIFs based on ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 are expected to inherit both merits of derivates independently from separate ZIF. Whereas the requirement of similar topological structure restricts its wide applica­ tion. During synthetic process, the precise operation (e.g., seed contents, metal ratio, stirring time/rate, etc.) is required to achieve the successful epitaxial growth. In these reported literatures, majority are found to be narrowly confined to ZIFs. In the future, more attempts should be paid to other types of MOFs.
## 3.3. Ion-exchange strategy
Ion-exchange is a classical solid liquid interface reaction occurring in the solid MOF precursors and chemical reagents in the solution. The simultaneous incorporation of various materials with distinct physicalchemical properties can be achieved by using this strategy. Since the coordination bonds in MOFs between metal nodes and organic ligands are weak yet reversible, these bonds within skeleton can be easily break to prepare multivariate MOF templates with different metal centers through ion exchange process [112–114]. The well-matched ion ex­ change and etching process occurred at the two-phase interfacial lead to the well-retained architecture and dimensions of original MOFs. Ac­ cording to the different type of the exchanged chemicals, ion-exchange reaction is classified into cation-exchange reaction and exchange pro­ cess involving anion.
Cation-exchange are intensively applied to introduce second metal into a single MOF framework to form mixed metal composites. For example, Co-ZIF-67 as a template was prepared first using a usual method and then reacted with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in ethanol solution to produce a Ni–Co MOF through cationic exchange reaction [115]. In this process, these released ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and partially oxidized ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ co-precipitated with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to produce Ni Co MOF. The bimetal MOF precursor still preserved a polyhedral morphology yet showed a rougher surface owing to the construction of nanosheets. Followed by a subsequent annealing treatment, hollow porous $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ -nanoboxes were obtained. Xu and colleagues demonstrated a cation-exchange approach for the conversion of ZIF-67 into $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/T i O_{2}}$ hollow polyhedrons [116]. Instead of using ethanol as solvent, they applied N, N-dimethyl formamide (DMF) for the $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ exchange reaction because ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions are unable to hydrolyze in basic DMF solution, leading to the cation exchange occurred on the surface of ZIF-67. It is worth mentioning that the cationic exchange took place relied on the lower electronegativity of ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{4+}$ than ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ . The ZIF-67 crystal was first dissolved and its bond between the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ cations and cluster was broke to re-bond with $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ cations stepwise. The DMF molecules gradually linked with Co ions since they still bounded to the cluster. Then, $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ -exchanged ZIF-67 skeleton was formed via the par­ tial replacement of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ by $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ . The ZIF-67 host showed adequate stability and flexibility to accommodate these exchanged $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ .
Polymetallic MOFs with single or multiple shelled nanostructures also can be fabricated by cationic exchange reaction. Starting from ZIF67, yolk-shelled ZIF-67/Ni − Co LDHs structures were synthesized and subsequent converted them into $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ double-shelled nanocages (DSNCs) by pyrolyzation [117]. Replacing $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ by $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , a series of single-shelled LDHs were effectively constructed, including ZIF-67/Mg Co LDHs and ZIF-67/Co Co LDHs, suggesting a strong universality of this strategy. In reference to similar route, the same group developed double-shelled $\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH}$ nanocages from single-shelled ZIF-67@LDH [118]. During this process, $\mathrm{Na_{2}M o O_{4}}$ was utilized to generate $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions through hydrolysis, and then $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ precipitated with ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ to yield ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ shell. Importantly, the as-prepared products maintained the polyhedral shape of parent ZIF67 core.
Additionally, anion-exchange reaction is also widely studied for the fabrication of novel-structured MOF hybrids. A series of multicompositional microboxes reported by Lou et al. represented a good example [119]. Using microsized Prussian blue (PB) $(\mathrm{Fe}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3})$ crystals as templates and $\mathrm{K_{2}S n O_{3}}$ as secondary metal source, hollow Fe $\mathrm{(OH)_{3}/S n O_{2}{\cdot}x H_{2}O}$ were synthesized via the ion exchange coupled with the hydrolysis reaction. ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{3}$ as conjugate base partially hydrolyzed to form oxide hydrate and created a proper alkaline environment, which induced PB cubes converted to ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes through $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions exchanged with $\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}^{4-}$ . Meanwhile, the in situ and stoichiometric precipitation of ${\bf S n O_{2}{\cdot}x H_{2}O}$ was facilitated by the consumption of $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ during the generation of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ shell, giving rise to the formation of Fe $(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ precursors. By annealing treatment of the asprepared samples, $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}/S n O_{2}}$ microboxes were produced. Using $\mathrm{Na_{2}G e O_{3}}$ and ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S i O}_{3}$ as the alkaline precursors, $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}/S i O_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}/G e O_{2}}$ microboxes were acquired based on the same approach. In another work, ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt M O}_{4}$ $[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}$ or W) was selected as alkaline pre­ cursors for the synthesis of $\mathrm{CoMO_{4}\mathrm{-Co(OH)_{2}}}$ nanocages [120]. At the first $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the color change of solution implied the initiation of re­ action between $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ and solid ZIF-67. When the refluxing time pro­ longed to $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , hollow $\mathrm{CoMoO_{4}\mathrm{-Co(OH)_{2}}}$ nanocages were fabricated. Chen et al. reported an anion-exchange modification to synthesize Fe doped cationic Co(II)-based MOF and then transformed into Fe-Co-N-C catalysts, where the $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ anions with charge balance blocked into its one-dimensional (1D) channels [121]. Through exchanging with $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ anions, $[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3}^{-}$ anions generated from $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ were introduced into the MOF channels, resulting in the formation of Fe/Co-coexisting MOF. After ion-exchange process, the integrity of MOF scaffold pre­ served well. Owing to the $\mathsf{I F e(C N)6]3^{-}}$ decomposition and the catalytic role of Fe atoms, Co NPs were wrapped by graphitic carbon layers, which were confirmed as one type of active sites for ORR.
Furthermore, the reactant concentration in the exchanged system has a remarkable effect on the composition and shell number. For instance, Lou and co-workers demonstrated a conversion of ZIF-67 into ZIF-67@amorphous- ${\mathrm{\Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{8}$ structure with the vanadium oxy­ triisopropoxide (VOT) as vanadium source [122]. Through an anionexchange reaction, 2-MIM anion in ZIF-67 skeleton was stepwise replaced by the $\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-}$ from VOT to generate amorphous $\mathrm{Co_{3}V_{2}O_{8}}$ shell. Particularly, the yolk-shelled ZIF-67 $@$ amorphous- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{8}{-}50$ with a thin shell and a large ZIF-67 core were obtained at $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ VOT amount. When the VOT concentration increased to $70~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}.$ , a similar yolk–shelled structure with a thicker shell and a shrunken core can be observed. As the VOT amount further increased to $120~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , the ZIF-67 core was entirely consumed to form hollow amorphous- ${\cdot\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}}$ nanoboxes. Accordingly, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Co_{3}V_{2}O_{8}}$ nanoboxes with triple shell and double shell and single-shelled $\mathrm{Co_{3}V_{2}O_{8}}$ were separately acquired after thermal annealing in air.
Ion-exchange strategy, as an energy-saving procedure, usually takes place at mild conditions. This method can form uniform nanocomposites in situ, which not only protects the original MOF structures from damage but also is scalable for large-scale production. For time-consuming ex­ change process (usually more than $24~\mathrm{h}$ ), microwave-assisted synthesis could be considered for rapid synthesis. Of note, the electronegativity of metal ions plays important role in the ion-exchange reaction. Through ion-exchange process, multiple shelled structures can be facilely ob­ tained from solid precursors and simple-component can also be con­ verted into multiple-component composites. Hence, the ion-exchange reaction may create great opportunity to fabricate complex nano­ materials in the light of both shell architecture and chemical composi­ tion. During the etching process, the concentration of exchanged substance is of great importance for the final morphology of poly­ metallic materials, further affecting their performance in reaction sys­ tem. Notably, the structural distortion may occur due to the
inappropriate exchanged time.
## 3.4. Guest species encapsulation strategy
The cavities in MOF skeletons endow them with adequate space to accommodate the naturally incompatible metal species, which can function as ideal platform for the preparation of multi-metallic guest@MOF precursors with new fascinating properties [123–125], whereas, it is difficult to perform by conventional methods. The rational immobilization of metal-containing species inside the MOF voids can purposely design functional nanomaterials with new fascinating prop­ erties. For example, Wang and co-workers reported a facile doublephase encapsulation approach (DPEA) to synthesize ${\mathrm{NiCoTU}}@{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}.$ MIL-101(Al) co-precursors (Fig. 8a) [15]. Herein, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Al) with large pores (ca. 2.9 and $3.4~\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) was chosen as host template for the accommodation of Ni-Co coordination compound and thiourea (TU). Benefiting from the immiscibility of the hydrophilic water-ethanol so­ lution containing Ni-Co/TU and hydrophobic solvent of $n$ -hexane, the as-proposed DPEA enabled the direct incorporation of the guest species into the internal pores of MOF template suspended in $n$ -hexane. Subse­ quently, a heat treatment was carried out to prepare the $\operatorname{Ni-Co-S}(\underline{{a}}\mathbf{G}/\mathbf{\Sigma}$ NSC samples. The encapsulation of the Ni-Co precursors and their uni­ form 3D distribution within the $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-101(Al) were conducive to forming small Ni-Co-S NPs and simultaneously induce the N and S active sites, resulting in excellent electrocatalytic activity. Similarly, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ mixed solution were dropwise pumped into the suspended MIL-101(Cr) with hexane as solvent [126]. Then, the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions encapsulated into MIL-101(Cr) pores were reduced by $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ to afford $\mathtt{C u N i@M I L-101(C r)}$ . During this process, Cu NPs were formed first and served as seeds to trigger the followed reduction of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions to produce CuNi NPs.
The integration of noble metal NPs into MOF pore channels and then calcinated at high temperature can afford the alloy structure, which not only gives a considerable enhancements for the physicochemical prop­ erties but also improves the utilization efficiency of noble metals [127]. For instance, $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}.67$ were prepared based on the hydrophobic/hy­ drophilic method [128]. Specifically, ZIF-67 with hydrophilic nature first dispersed in a hydrophobic solvent of $n$ -hexane, and then Pt NPs were adsorbed into the MOF pores owing to its hydrophilic affinity towards ZIF-67. Followed by hydrogen reduction and covered with tannic acid (TA), Pt@ZIF-67@TA precursors were produced. After py­ rolysis, the final PtCo/Co@NHPCC catalysts were obtained, where the unique alloy structure and ultrafine PtCo NPs improved the inherent activity, stability as well as utilization efficiency of Pt. The hollow porous carbon support with nitrogen dopant and high surface area was contributed to the excellent catalytic property. The concentration of solvent is important in the synthetic procedure. A mass of $n$ -hexane was help to disperse ZIF-67 particles and generate the repulsive outer hy­ drophobic environment around Co-ZIF, promoting the adsorption pro­ cess of metal precursors. Recently, the Au and Pd ions guests were trapped into ZIF-67 pores by a de novo method, followed by reduction into bimetallic Au-Pd NPs in $\mathrm{{NaBH}_{4}}$ solution [129]. A further thermal treatment under air was employed to yield $\mathrm{Au_{x}P d@C o_{3}O_{4}}$ cages with an active interface between cobalt oxide and Au-Pd alloyed NPs (Fig. 8b-d). In this work, ZIF-67 served as a template to separate cobalt clusters while not catalyst. The organic ligands in ZIF-67 were sacrificed to make the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions close, leading to the formation of the efficient interface with powerful interaction. It is noting that Au and Pd tended to form bimetallic NPs instead of alloy NPs after reduction process because both two noble metals possessed multiple oxidation states in the AuPd@ZIF67 composites.
Aside from metal NPs, polyoxometalates (POMs) are widely utilized to encapsulate into MOF pores to construct guest $@$ host template. POMs are a class of metal-oxygen anion clusters abounding in transition metal source (e.g., Mo, W, V), which have emerged as promising precursors to synthesize the active Mo- or W-containing catalysts. MOFs embedded with POMs endow the composites with greatly enhancement in perfor­ mance owing to the combination of individual advantages [130,131]. As an example, Li et al. conceived an effective strategy to design $\mathbf{PMo}_{12}.$ incorporated MIL-100 (Fe) precursors, which responsible for the source of Fe, Mo and P simultaneously [132]. In the mixture of $\mathrm{H_{3}P M o_{12}O_{40}}.$ , Fe salts and organic linkers, $\mathbf{PMo}_{12}$ was formed accompanied with the selfassembly process of MIL-100 (Fe). This method simplified the synthetic procedures and reduced the energy consumption. The obtained $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –100$ (Fe) showed polyhedral morphology, similar to parent MIL-100 (Fe), suggesting the structure still maintained intact after encapsulated operation. Followed by calcination with melamine, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}/$ $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ -containing N, P co-doped graphitic carbon was obtained (Fig. 8e).
Without the addition of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{P}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}_{12},}\end{array}$ sole MIL-100 (Fe) derived $\mathtt{F e}_{3}\mathtt{C}_{\rVert}\mathtt{C}$ exhibited a lower graphitic degree than that of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}C/M o_{2}C@N P G C}$ (Fig. 8f, g). In another case, Tang and co-workers demonstrated a novel solid-phase hot-pressing method to fabricate POMs-based $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Zn}$ -ZIFs precursors on carbon cloth (CC) without the usage of binders [133]. In brief, $\mathbf{PMo}_{12}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions, organic ligands and polyethylene glycol with a little ethylene glycol were grinded together to produce purple pastes. Later, the pastes were deposited on CC using an electric iron at moderate environment to form $\mathbf{PMo}_{12}$ -Co/Zn-ZIFs/CC compos­ ites. Subsequently, the $\mathrm{CoP/MoP@NC/CC}$ with unique structure and large surface area was prepared through the phosphidation. Compared to the traditional solvent method, this method has many advantages of rapid synthesis, mild reaction conditions, non-pollution and high yield.
Based on the ordered porous structure of MOFs, the guest species encapsulation strategy is effectively proposed. The successful imple­ mentation of such method relies heavily on the well-matched pore size of MOF template with molecular/ion dimension. Owing to the geometric confinement effect of MOFs, guest species confined in MOFs after calcination could avoid the particle aggregation and these obtained metal NPs may be small-sized, uniformly embedded within the carbon matrix with a high density, thus showing an improved reactivity for specific application. Besides, MOF scaffolds can serve as a multi-spatialcompartmental nanoreactor that allows physically compartmentalize multiple active species in a single MOF nanoparticle for facilitating mass transportation. However, the selection of the type and content of guest species encapsulated into the MOF hole is narrow due to the intrinsic feature of pore structure of MOFs, which hindered the widespread usage of this strategy.
## 3.5. Solution impregnation strategy
Impregnation method has been widely applied in industrial field. Generally, MOF precursors are subjected to calcination and then the obtained derivatives are infiltrated by metal solution followed by posttreatment to generate the final multi-metal nanomaterials with unique properties [134]. Unlike other strategies, this synthetic method is to introduce foreign metal species into the skeletons of MOF derivatives instead of pristine MOFs. Accordingly, more meticulous operation and mild conditions are needed to avoid the structural damage.
Zhou et al. synthesized Co-Fe bimetallic composites using impreg­ nation method with iron acetylacetonate as Fe source [135]. Starting from ZIF-67, Co/N-C dodecahedral nanocages (DNCs) were fabricated by high-temperature calcination. Then the obtained ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{-C}}$ samples were mixed with iron acetylacetonate at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to produce C $\mathbf{\Gamma_{0-}F e_{3}O_{4}/N}.$ - C DNCs, in which $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs grew on the inside and outside surfaces of Co/N-C. After annealing with S power, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N\mathrm{-}C}$ DNCs were finally synthesized. The structure of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N\mathrm{-C}}$ DNCs still remained integrity without obvious destroy after solution impregnation process and twice high-temperature calcination. The bimetallic Co8FeS8 NPs were evenly embedded within porous hollow N-doped carbon matrix, leading to the synergy effect for remarkable performance. Xu and co-workers prepared Fe-introduced $\mathrm{Zn}@\mathrm{Co-MOF}$ composites by two-step method [136]. At first, a core $@$ shell structured ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{ZIF-67}}$ was synthesized by precise epitaxial growth. After that, guest Fe ions from iron nitrate were intro­ duced into $Z n@C o$ -MOFs host through wet impregnation to obtain the final samples.
Li et al. reported a general route to fabricate tri-metallic nanorods using bimetallic FeNi-MOFs as templates [137]. The bi-MOF precursors were transformed into $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}C_{x}}$ nanorods by thermal annealing under argon atmosphere. In this process, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions were reduced into alloyed FeNi NPs by the carbonaceous species came from trimesic acid ligands. The as-obtained $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}C_{x}}$ nanorods were dipped into the aqueous solution of $\mathrm{Na_{2}P d C l_{4}}$ to form $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}C_{x}{\mathrm{-}}P d_{y}}$ with claviform shape. During which, $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ ions reacted with L-Ascorbic acid on the surface of $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}C_{x}}$ nanorods, leading to the uniformed dispersion of crystalline Pd NPs on the outer carbon layer. Similarly, $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF-derived $\mathtt{C u/C}$ precursor powders were slowly added into a chloroplatinate solution for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ to synthesize a core-shell structured $\mathrm{Cu@Pt/C}$ catalyst [138]. Note that, even a little amount of Cu species in $\mathrm{Cu@Pt/C}$ can induce more active sites to greatly improve the electrochemical activity compared to pure $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Mandegarzad et al. reported a novel catalyst based on bimetallic Cu-Pd NPs supported on nanoporous carbon com­ posites (Cu-Pd/NPCC) through a three-step process [139]. MOF-199 $(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2},$ $\mathtt{B T C}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylate) was selected as pre­ cursor and synthesized by an electrochemical method. Further direct carbonization under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere was performed to obtain $\mathtt{C u/N P C C}$ . After the addition of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ solution, Cu-Pd/NPCC was fabricated via galvanic replacement of Cu atoms by Pd (II) ions. The Pd NPs with size of $50{-}90~\mathrm{nm}$ were well dispersed on the porous network of the Cu/NPCC. Benefiting from the interaction effect of bimetallic composite due to diminishing of d-band center of Pd by Cu component, the catalysts showed excellent electrocatalytic activity towards HER.
Recently, Guo et al. demonstrated two routes involving one-step and post-treated method to construct $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ -based bimetal oxides [140]. In detail, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{C u O}$ catalysts were synthesized by thermolysis of Cecentered MOFs followed by loading CuO species using impregnation method. Differently, $\mathsf{C e-C u-O_{x}}$ catalysts were obtained from the direct carbonization of bimetal $\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{C u})$ -MOFs. Surprisingly, both of them pre­ sented a similar rod-like structure and pore size distribution as well as comparable surface area. However, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{C u O}$ sample displayed inten­ sively higher lattice oxygen percentage of $60.8\%$ than that of ${\mathsf{C e-C u-O_{x}}}$ of $46.0\%$ , giving rise to superior activity in CO oxidation. The different synthetic approach led to varying synergetic interaction between $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and $\mathtt{C u O}$ species, and thereby affected the defect sites, redox ability, and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ -CO adsorption strength. It is worth mentioning that the extremely low quality of CuO species, homogeneous dispersion and integrated into the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ lattice resulted in the absence of characteristic diffraction peaks of $\mathtt{C u O}$ in XRD pattern of $\mathtt{C e O_{2}-C u O}$ and $_\mathrm{Ce-Cu-O_{X}}$ samples.
The MOF derivatives are completely and uniformly immersed in metal-containing solution by using solution impregnation strategy. After thermal treatment, the metal NPs could homogeneously grow on the inside and outside of MOF derivatives. Although solution impregnation has been proven to be efficient, the complex and tedious operation involved multiple steps in synthesis may bring a possibility of structural failure, which could increase the cost and reduce the reproducibility of samples. It is highly significant to carefully manipulate the infiltrating reaction between MOF derivatives and foreign metal source through controlling the reaction conditions. Besides, in some cases, the slight collapse of pore structure of MOF after pyrolysis makes it difficult to immersing metal species into pores. The size compatibility between derivative and metal component is important. In the future, more wide and deep investigations along with the solution impregnation strategy are highly needed to explore facile yet efficient route to prepare multi­ component materials.
## 3.6. Combination with extraneous substrates
MOF precursors combined with extraneous pre-designed metal sub­ strates to fabricate the multi-component materials have gathered intensive interests [141,142]. These distinctive substrates possessing diverse morphology are deem to be favorable supports for MOFs. The formed intimate physical and chemical contact between them offers opportunity for generation of new properties [143,144]. Although some metal component can play a role solely, the results still leave much to be desired. Integrating with functional MOF-based materials is an effective way to improve their performance. In the research of Kuang, bimetal Cu, Co-embedded N-enriched carbon matrix $(\mathsf{C u C o@N C})$ catalysts were synthesized by using ZIF-67 on $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ nanowires as co-precursors (Fig. 9a-c) [145]. ZIF-67 was in situ grown on the as-obtained Cu $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanowires through a self-assembly reaction. Then, the assynthesized $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ composites were transformed into the final $\operatorname{CuCo@NC}$ by thermal treatment under inert gas at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by acid etch. The different tolerance for decomposition temperature of ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ provided a confinement effect of Co and Cu metals within N-doped carbons, avoiding the self-aggregation and irreversible fusion of Cu NPs (Fig. 9d). Similarly, a certain amount of $\mathtt{C u O}$ nano­ sheets were inserted into the dodecahedral ZIF-8 to construct CuO-ZIF-8 composites and then evolved into Cu, Zn embedded porous carbon frameworks $({\mathsf{C u Z n}}(\emptyset{\mathsf{C}})$ [146]. The $\mathtt{C u O}$ nanosheets were converted to small $\mathtt{C u_{2}O}$ NPs at high temperature. With the increase of $\mathtt{C u O}$ amounts, the BET specific surface area of $\mathrm{CuZn}@\mathsf{C}$ was decreasing owing to the pore blockage of Cu species. In the research of Zhao, carbon nanotube fibers (CNTFs) were mixed with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ solution for the prepa­ ration of $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}$ nanowire array (NA) on the CNTFs substrates [147]. Then, the synthesized $\mathrm{CNTFs@ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}$ served as $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions source and a skeleton for in situ growth of $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF, leading to the formation of core-shell structured $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}$ NA@Zn/Co-ZIF. The welloriented hybrid arrays with a rod-like morphology of $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}$ $\mathrm{NA@Zn/Co-ZIF}$ possessed a uniform length of several micrometers. After oxidation and sulfurization, $\mathrm{CNTFs@ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}@Z n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{-}S$ hybrid arrays were produced.
Nanotube structure with large surface area and well-defined inner channels could be an ideal support for the in-situ growth of MOFs [148–150]. For example, $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanotubes with an overall diameter of $85\ \mathrm{nm}$ were first prepared by a hydrothermal method and then used as supports for synthesis of 1D hierarchical hollow $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ hybrids (Fig. 9e) [151]. Relying on the electrostatic interactions, the $\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ nanotubes with negative charge absorbed ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions onto outer layer and thus formed core–shell structured $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ (Fig. 9f, j and $g,\mathbf{k})$ . Upon adding $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , the hollow NiCo-LDH nanocages gradually formed on the $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ surface by etching reaction, resulting in $\mathtt{M n O}_{2}@$ ­ NiCo-LDH precursors (Fig. 9h, l). After sulfuration, the final 1D hollow nanomaterials were obtained (Fig. 9i, m). Remarkably, the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions and thioacetamide (TAA) concentration as well as the hydrothermal reaction time were importance to the structure and property of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{MnO}_{2}@\mathbf{NiCo}-}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{LDH}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ . Based on the similar synthetic route, hierarchical structured bimetallic $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@N i_{2}P}$ -CoP/NF hybrids [152] and ternary $\mathrm{CoO/Co_{3}O_{4}/}$ ${\mathsf{C o M o O}}_{4}$ composites [153] were fabricated by using $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowire array and ${\mathsf{C o M o O}}_{4}$ nanorods as supports, respectively.
Extraneous substrates containing metal compositions combined with MOFs can effectively construct polymetallic hybrids. Each component possesses a special function that can conquer the defects of the indi­ vidual counterparts and plays unique role in the composites, thus generating a powerful synergistic effect for enhanced performance. In many cases, the integrated substrates not only serve as secondary metal source but also self-sacrificing templates for oriented-growth of morphology. However, this strategy involved pre-design steps is a timeconsuming process and may increase the production cost. In situ growth for MOFs and foreign species by one-step method is more expected.
## 4. Electrocatalytic applications
The growing energy demand prompts the exploration and develop­ ment of efficient materials for chemical energy conversion process. Owing to the rational design and unique properties, MOF-derived multimetal nanomaterials have been highlighted as electrocatalysts for energy-conversion reactions [154–156]. Besides, MOF-derived electro­ catalysts with well-designed structure also can exhibit bi- or trifunctional features, endowing them promising potential in nextgeneration energy devices such as rechargeable metal-air batteries and overall water splitting. Specifically, electrochemical water splitting in­ volves two half reactions: HER on the cathode electrode and OER on the anode. Besides, ORR is crucial in full cell and metal–air batteries [157]. In this section, the application of MOF-derived multi-metal nano­ materials as catalysts in energy-conversion electrochemical reactions are discussed, including the oxygen reduction reaction, oxygen evolu­ tion reaction and hydrogen evolution reaction. The catalytic perfor­ mances of MOF-derived multi-metallic nanomaterials for electrocatalytic applications have been summarized in Table 2.
## 4.1. ORR
Enormous efforts have been devoted to develop ORR catalysts, which can exhibit high current density at low overpotential. The ORR process proceeds through multiple steps of oxygen-containing intermediates adsorbed on catalyst surface, like associative, dissociative, or perox­ ospecies formation [158,159]. Several two-electron and four-electron reactions control the overall efficiency of the catalytic systems. Selec­ tive two-electron reduction produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ whereas four-electron reduction leads to the formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . In most cases, the fourelectron route is more preferred owing to the superior efficiency. As is well-known, noble metal Pt is recognized as the benchmark electro­ catalyst for this process. However, the high cost and the natural scarcity of Pt still hamper its large-scale implementation. Dealloying of Pt with transition metal to expose increased active sites and concurrently reduce the usage of Pt is an effective approach to ensure the superior ORR ac­ tivity. For example, Chen et al. utilized ZIF-67 as precursor to develop PtCo bimetallic nanoparticles anchored in N-doped carbon capsules PtCo/Co@NHPCC [128]. $\mathrm{H_{2}P t C l_{6}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ acted as guest molecules were introduced into the pores of Co-based MOFs. Followed by hydrogen reduction and pyrolysis process, the final $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{PtCo/Co@NHPCC}}$ was pro­ duced. The as-synthesized catalysts showed an excellent ORR perfor­ mance, with a mass activity that was 5.5 and 13.5 times higher than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and Pt black, respectively. Besides, it was exciting to find that $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{PtCo/Co@NHPCC}}$ catalyst achieved a considerably improved durability in terms of both electrochemically active surface area and mass activity, in comparison with commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and Pt black cata­ lysts. The structural features (such as alloy structure, good dispersivity and fine particle size) as well as the carbon matrix are responsible for the outstanding electrocatalytic activity.
Downsizing the noble metal particles to expose more available atoms on the surface is another approach to make Pt-based catalysts more affordable. Atomically dispersed metal catalysts with high atom effi­ ciency have sparked new interest in electrocatalysis [160–162]. In the research of Yao, a locally distributed atomic Pt-Co nitrogen carbonbased catalyst (A-CoPt-NC) was reported [163]. The obtained catalyst only contained a small content of Co $({\sim}1.72\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ and a very little Pt $({\sim}0.16~\mathrm{wt\%})$ . Unexpectedly, it displayed extremely high activities for
ORR in alkaline media, delivering mass activity 267 times greater than that of the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. No noticeable decay in activity was observed after a $240~\mathrm{min}$ electrochemical durability test. Furthermore, the A-CoPt-NC showed a high selectivity for the 4-electron pathway in ORR, which can be ascribed to the atomic interaction of $\mathrm{Pt}-\mathsf{C o}$ .
Considering the agglomeration and degradation of Pt-based catalyst during annealing and cycling tests, constructing core-shell structure by combing noble metal and transition metal is highly desirable, because it can increase the catalytic efficiency and simultaneously maintain the structural stability. As an example, a quasi-Pt-allotrope catalyst, comprising of hollow $\mathrm{Pt}_{3}\mathrm{Co}$ core and N-doped carbon embedding singleatom Pt shell $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{H-PtCo@Pt_{1}N-C})$ was prepared (Fig. 10a) [164]. When tested in $0.1_{\mathrm{~M}}\mathrm{HCl}0_{4}$ solution, the mass activity of H-PtCo@Pt1N-C was $1.2\mathrm{mA}\upmu\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}$ at $0.9\mathrm{V}$ , corresponding to 8, 10 and 3.4 times greater than those of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(0.15\ \mathrm{mA}\ \upmu\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1\cdot}$ ), Pt/NPC $(0.12\ \mathrm{mA}\ \upmu\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}.$ ) and PtCo/NPC $(0.35\mathrm{\mA\\upmug_{Pt}^{-1}})$ , respectively (Fig. 10b). After 10,000 cycle tests, the catalyst presented stable activity without any fade, showing the excel­ lent durability towards ORR (Fig. 10c). Benefiting from the synergistic effect between the single Pt atom/N-doped carbon shell and hollow PtCo alloy core, the $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}{\mathsf{C o}}(\underline{{a}}\mathrm{Pt}_{1}\mathrm{N}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited efficient and stable ORR activities in various organic electrolytes, suggesting its huge potential in next-generation lithium–air batteries.
Owing to the inherent defects of noble metals in reserve and cost, searching for earth-abundant transition metal alternatives with favor­ able activity and durability is an attractive task for scientific community. In the research of Hassan, $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ nanocomposite fibers were con­ structed by electrospinning method followed by a simple sintering process and utilized as ORR electrocatalyst [165]. The bimetal oxides with mixed valence provided numerous accessible active sites. The halfwave potential of $0.53{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the initial potential of $0.59{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathsf{C}/$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ were more positive than single $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ and carbon free $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ . The incorporated carbon can greatly promote the electron transfer, thus imposing a prominent contribution to the electrocatalytic activity. Meng et al. reported a hierarchically structured porous $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-Zn}.$ - N-C electrocatalyst for ORR by using ZnCo-ZIF as precursors [166]. The as-prepared HC-5Co95Zn catalysts exhibited excellent half-wave po­ tential $(0.78{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE) and diffusion limiting current density $(5.7\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , which were close to those of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst in acidic solution (Fig. 10d). Besides, the resulted HC-5Co95Zn presented a lower Tafel slope of $66\mathrm{mVdec}^{-1}$ and better long-term stability compared with that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ of $75\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ . After 30,000 s stability test, the normalized current of HC-5Co95Zn decreased $12.6\%$ , while $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ faded by $36.1\%$ after 20,000 s chronoamperometric response (Fig. 10e). The hierarchical porous architecture was responsible for the remarkable ORR catalytic activity. Wang and co-workers developed hierarchical porous N,Scodoped carbon immobilized with Co-Ni-sulphide nanoparticles (Ni$\mathbf{Co-S@G/NSC})$ from $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}{\cdot}101(\$ (Al) [167]. Benefiting from the distinctive nanoarchitectures and the synergetic effect between the active metal species, the Ni-Co- $\mathsf{S}@\mathsf{G}/\mathsf{N S C}$ delivered an ultrahigh power density of $261.3\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ and high specific capacity density of 2947 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , as well as comparable onset potential (0.94 V vs. RHE) and half-wave potential $_{\mathrm{0.86~V}}$ vs. RHE) to the commercial Pt/C catalyst in ORR.
## 4.2. OER
As the reverse reaction to the ORR, the OER is vital to many energyconversion devices as well. Similar to ORR, OER is a complicated fourelectron process containing multistep elementary reactions, where $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation with different reaction mechanism under acidic and alkaline conditions. Actually, OER normally occurs as a rate determining step due to the intrinsically sluggish kinetics stemming from multistep proton-coupled four-electron transfer process related to the H–O frac­ ture and O–O bonding [168,169]. At present, noble-metal-based cata­ lysts (e.g., Ir, $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ are considered as the benchmark electrocatalysts towards OER. Utilizing MOF-derived multi-metal nanomaterials to replace precious metal catalysts for OER have been widely reported. Owing to the high activity and low cost, transitionmetal-oxides has recently attracted significant attention. As an example, 3D $\mathrm{Ni_{x}C o_{3-x}O_{4-y}}$ nanocages were synthesized using Ni-doped ZIF-67 as precursors and investigated their electrocatalytic OER char­ acteristics [170]. Substitution of Ni into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structure helped to improve the inherent catalytic activity. By virtue of the presence of nonstoichiometry and low coordination metal sites, which could reduce the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ adsorption energy and promote the conductivity of catalyst, the $\mathrm{Ni_{x}C o_{3-x}O_{4-y}}$ showed enhanced OER activity. The resulting NiCo-1 (Ni/ ${\mathsf{C o}}=1$ ) exhibited a lower overpotential of $320\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ and a lower Tafel slope of $53\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1}$ . The porous structure and rich redox centers were contributed to the excellent OER performance and made it a competitive electrocatalyst for water oxidation reaction.
Similarly, Ni–Co mixed oxide cages derived from Ni–Co PBA cages were developed as electrocatalysts for OER [48]. The as-prepared cat­ alysts displayed an outstanding performance towards OER with a small overpotential of $0.38{\mathrm{~V~}}$ at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Besides, the catalytic activity of Ni–Co mixed oxide cages was no obvious loss after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ of the electrochemical reactions in alkaline medium, showing its good durability. In addition to transition metal oxides, transition metal sulfides also showed their improved catalytic activity for OER [171,172]. Recently, ultrathin $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/C o S}$ nanoparticles grown on carbon nanotubes (CNTs) skeleton $(\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}}/\mathrm{CoS}@\mathrm{CNT})$ were devel­ oped as OER catalyst, which delivered a low overpotential of $278\mathrm{mV}$ for $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a very small Tafel slope of $49{\mathrm{~mV~dec}}^{-1}$ as well as good long-run stability [173]. The excellent activity was attributed to the introduction of highly conductive CNTs and the homogenously distrib­ uted $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/C o S}$ nanoparticles.
Aside from multi-metal oxides and sulfides, transition-metal-based phosphides also possessed promising potential for OER due to their free noble metal, favorable activity and hydrogenaselike catalytic mechanism. Among them, Co based bimetallic phosphides have been proved as cost-effective OER electrocatalysts. Li et al. fabricated carbon incorporated homobimetallic (CoNi/C, CoCu/C, CoMn/C) phosphide electrocatalyst for OER [174]. The as-obtained CoNi/C-HHPNC showed highest OER activity with a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at over­ potentials of about $267\mathrm{mV}$ , much lower than that of the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $366\mathrm{mV})$ [175] and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{338~mV}$ ) [176]. CoMnP/C and CoCuP/C-HHPNC also delivered lower potentials of 262 and $283~\mathrm{mV}$ respectively to reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . In addition, CoNi/C-HHPNC retained $98.6\%$ of the initial current density after $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ at the overpotential of 280 $\mathrm{\mV}$ , indicating its long-term stability in regard to the intrinsic func­ tionality. The density functional theory (DFT) calculations demon­ strated that the homogenously introduced secondary metals and the incorporated carbon effectively regulated the electronic structure, thus collaboratively resulting in the low kinetic energy barrier for the OER. Using bimetal CoFe-PBA as platform, bimetal cobalt iron phosphides $(\mathsf{C o}_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{F e}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{P})$ with uniform metal distribution were achieved [177]. By continuously optimizing the ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{F e}}$ ratio, the best catalytic activity was observed at the Co/Fe ratio of 1.63, which exhibited a small over­ potentials of 230 and $268~\mathrm{mV}$ at 10 and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, exceeding most of the reported transition-metal phosphides. The supe­ rior OER activity could ascribed to the more exposed active sites caused by small size of ${\bf C o}_{1-\bf x}\mathrm{Fe}_{\bf x}{\bf P}$ and the electronic modulation due to the Fe doping. Lou et al. fabricated carbon incorporated Ni–Co mixed metal phosphide nanoboxes (NiCoP/C) by two-step methods [178]. Specif­ ically, ZIF-67 nanocubes were synthesized and then reacted with Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ to form ZIF $\bar{\cdot}67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ layered double hydroxide (LDH) nano­ boxes. After phosphidation with ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2},$ the nanocomposite NiCoP/C nanoboxes were produced. Benefiting from the structural and compo­ sitional merits, the as-prepared catalysts showed enhanced electro­ chemical performance and long-term stability for OER, which required an overpotential of $330~\mathrm{mV}$ to attain a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 4.3. HER
Transition-metal-based nitrides have drawn intense attention recently because the introduced $\mathbf{N}$ atoms with negatively charge can modify the d-band density of the parent metals, making the electronic structures of metal nitrides similar to group VIII noble metals [185–187]. Guo et al. prepared porous rodlike cobalt nickel bimetal nitrides $(\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{y}N)}$ by nitridation from MOF-74 [188]. Tanks to the structural advantages, uniformed metal dispersion and electron modu­ lation effect. The resulting catalysts exhibited a superior catalytic ability for HER at all $\mathfrak{p H}$ values. The optimized $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{N}$ delivered over­ potentials of $92.0\mathrm{mV}$ and $102.6\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ in acidic and alkaline media, respectively. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{N}$ presented ignorable loss of current density after $24\mathrm{h}$ test, indicating its excellent electrochemical stability in acidic environment. Recently, tri­ metallic Fe/Co/Cu-based phosphides decorated with N-doped carbongraphene $({\mathrm{FeCoCuP}}@{\mathrm{NC}})$ were fabricated through a facile two-step approach [189]. When used as HER electrocatalyst, $\mathtt{F e C o C u P@N C}$ showed superior catalytic performance with low overpotential of 80 and $169\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and low Tafel slopes of 47.6 and $48.8\:\mathrm{mV}\:\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\cal O}_{4}$ and 1.0 M KOH, respectively. These values were better than those of FeCoCu $@\mathrm{NC}$ without phosphorization, illustrating the positive effect of phosphorization on the HER performance. Besides, the N-doped carbon-graphene matrix, the for­ mation of heterojunctions between different phosphides as well as the cooperation effect between active phosphides were beneficial to the improved HER activity.
## 4.4. Bi−/tri-functional electrocatalysts
In fact, the practical electrochemical devices usually involve more than one process. For example, the metal–air batteries are composed of OER and ORR, and the overall water splitting is related to HER and OER. Considering that, developing multifunctional electrocatalysts performed well in HER, OER and ORR and possess excellent durability is favorable. Multi-metallic nanomaterials derived from MOFs with particular prop­ erty can provide synergy and are expected to replace precious metals to meet the demand of further development. As an example, Zhao et al. reported a facile synthesis strategy for bimetal phosphide nanotubes for overall water splitting [190]. By adjusting the molar ratio of Co/Ni atoms in MOF-74 precursor, a series of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathrm{{x}}}{\mathrm{{Ni}}}_{\mathrm{{y}}}{\mathrm{{P}}}$ electrocatalysts were fabricated through low-temperature phosphorization. Among them, the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co_{4}N i_{1}P}$ with similar morphology to precursor possessed a rare form of nanotubes, and exhibited favorable dispersal of the active sites. The catalysts presented a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at over­ potentials of $129\ \mathrm{mV}$ for HER and $245~\mathrm{mV}$ for OER, respectively. Notably, an electrolyzer using $\mathsf{C o}_{4}\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{P}$ as both the anode and cathode catalyst in alkaline medium afforded a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at a voltage of $1.59\mathrm{~V~}$ , which could compete with the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ counterparts. Moreover, $\mathsf{C o}_{4}\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{P}$ catalysts showed long-term stability with no obvious loss of after $50\mathrm{h}$ continuous operation.
In another case, the ultrafine and monodispersed NiCoZnP nano­ clusters distributed on the ultrathin NC nanosheets (NiCoZnP/NC) were successfully fabricated as binder-free catalysts for overall water splitting [191]. Owing to the small size of NiCoZnP and the hierarchical nano­ architectures of NC, the catalysts showed large surface area and abun­ dant active sites, thus leading to the enhanced electrocatalytic properties. The self-supported NiCoZnP/NC nanosheets displayed high activity for HER with low overpotential of $74~\mathrm{mV}$ and OER with over­ potential of $228~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in 1.0 M KOH solution. Also, a low potential of $1.54\mathrm{~V~}$ to reach $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ and excellent durability with a little deactivation during $85\mathrm{h}$ of continuous electrolysis testing were achieved by NiCoZnP/NC.
Designing non-precious electrocatalysts with unique hetero­ structures is important to improve the electrocatalytic activity. For example, N-doped carbon-decorated $\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{F e C o P}$ yolk-shelled micropolyhedrons were prepared by phosphorization from ZIF-6 $7@$ Co-FePBAs (Fig. 11a) [192]. Benefiting from the yolk-shell construction and N-doped carbon matrix, the aggregation degree of CoP was large miti­ gated, thus leading to the high specific surface area and more exposed active sites. Besides, the synergistic effect between the yolk-shell structure and N-doped carbon also helped to optimize the electronic structure. To drive the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , the overpotentials of $\mathrm{CoP}@\mathrm{FeCoP}$ only required 141 and $238\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline electrolyte for HER and OER, respectively, lower than those of CoP/NC, ZIF-67 micropolyhedrons and ZIF-67 $@$ Co-Fe PBA (Fig. 11b and c). When used as a two-electrode for water splitting, it needed a low cell voltage of $1.68\mathrm{V}$ to achieve a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , much better than that of $\mathrm{RuO_{2}}||\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}$ electrolyzer, and exhibited good long-term stability after 20 h (Fig. 11d and e). Recently, a unique core–shell structure of $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ laminated with $\mathbf{MoSe}_{2}$ derived from Co-MOF (MOF- $\mathrm{CoSe_{2}}@\mathrm{MoSe_{2}}$ was successfully fabricated [193]. MOF- $\mathrm{CoSe_{2}}@\mathrm{MoSe_{2}}$ core–shell structure was directly grown on a flexible CC substrate and avoided the usage of binder. The as-prepared core-shell structured CC/MOF- $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}@\mathsf{M o S e}_{2}$ demonstrated a low overpotentials of 109.87 and $183.81~\mathrm{mV}$ for the HER and OER in alkaline media, respectively, and a low voltage of 1.53 V for a two-electrode water electrolysis, outperformed those of CC/MOF and CC/MoSe2.
As for bifunctional oxygen electrocatalysts, Chen et al. reported amorphous bimetallic $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanoparticles embedded in N-doped reduced graphene oxide (rGO) by using Fe-Co PBAs as precursors [194]. The as-obtained amorphous $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}0_{\mathrm{x}}$ showed superior OER activity with an overpotential of $257~\mathrm{mV}$ for $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a Tafel slope of $30.1~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}$ , as well as excellent ORR activity with a large limiting current density of $-5.25\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0.6\mathrm{V}$ . More importantly, a Zn-air battery delivered a peak power density of $86\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , a specific ca­ pacity of $756\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{Zn}}^{-1}$ (corresponding to an energy density of 904 W h $\mathbf{kg}_{\mathrm{Zn}}^{-1}.$ ) and good long-term stability over $120\mathrm{h}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . Using the bamboo-like nanofibers composites of Ni–Co-BTC mixed with poly­ acrylonitrile as precursors, NiCo- $0.8@\mathbb{N}$ -CNFs-800 were developed as bifunctional catalysts for ORR and OER [195]. Owing to a highly dopedN content, uniform dispersion of NiCo alloy and decentralized $\mathsf{N i C o-N_{x}}$ active sites, the NiCo- $\phantom{-}0.8\textcircled{a}$ N-CNFs-800 performed an excellent elec­ trocatalytic performance. Specially, the ORR onset potential on NiCo$0.8@\mathrm{N-CNFs-}800$ was 0.91 V and the half-wave potential was $0.82\mathrm{~V~}$ . Under the OER condition, NiCo- $0.8@\mathrm{N}$ -CNFs-800 achieved a minimum potential of $1.61~\mathrm{V}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . In recent, atomically isolated binary Co-Ni sites anchored on N-doped hollow
CNTs (CoNi-SAs/NC) were constructed by pyrolysis of dopamine-coated CoNi-MOF [196]. By virtue of the rich single-atom active sites and cooperation effect between diatomic structure in porous conductive carbon matrix, the as-synthesized CoNi-SAs/NC showed superior cata­ lytic activity for ORR and OER by presenting low overpotential, high electron transfer number and excellent reversibility in alkaline electro­ lyte, outperforming many non-precious catalysts. In comparison with single monometallic Ni-N and CoNi counterparts, DFT calculations revealed that the accessibility of atomically dispersed Co-Ni sites and the synergy of dual metal-N structure (Co-Ni-N) were conducing to reducing the energetic barrier and boosting reaction kinetics.
Aside from bifunctional electrocatalysts, the cost-efficient and du­ rable trifunctional heterostructure electrocatalysts for HER, OER and ORR are crucial to overall water splitting and regenerative metal-air batteries. In the research of Yuan, binary FeNi phosphide coupled with N, P-modified carbon nanosheets (FeNiP/NPCS) were synthesized through carbonization-phosphorization method [197]. The structural advantages and optimized electronic structure of FeNiP/NPCS were responsible for the exceptional activity of overall water splitting and rechargeable $Z\mathbf{n}$ -air batteries, achieving excellent performance in these energy conversion and storage devices. In another case, $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ nano­ sheets vertically aligned on the $\scriptstyle{C o}$ -decorated N-doped carbon poly­ hedron $(\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}/\mathbf{Co}@\mathbf{NC})$ were successfully constructed [198]. Benefiting from the powerful interfacial effect from $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanosheets and $\mathsf{C o@N C}$ polyhedron as well as the reciprocally permeated structure, the intrinsic properties and accessible active sites were greatly improved, resulting in high electrocatalytic performance and good durability for HER, OER and ORR (Fig. 12a-f). Using $\mathrm{Mo_{2}C/C o@N C}$ as the electrodes, $\mathrm{Mo_{2}C}/\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{NC}.$ based Zn-air battery could efficiently motivate the electrochemical water splitting. Recently, Lee et al. CoP-N-doped carbon@NiFeP nano­ flakes (CoP-NC@NFP) were developed as multifunctional catalysts for water splitting and zinc-air battery [199]. The abundant active catalytic sites in CoP-NC@NFP were contributed to the excellent charge-transfer kinetics and electrocatalytic activity, with an overpotential of 270 and $162\mathrm{mV}$ for OER and HER, respectively, and a Tafel slope of $46\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ for ORR (Fig. 12g). With respective to overall water splitting device, CoP-NC@NFP required a cell voltage of $1.57\mathrm{V}$ to attain a current den­ sity of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 12h). Besides, zinc-air batteries assembled with CoP-NC@NFP as the air–cathode presented high power density of 93 $\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 12i). These multifunctional electrocatalysts free of noble metals with remarkable activity and excellent stability opens a practical possibility for clean energy production and storage.
Optimizing the performance of electrocatalysts requires atomic-scale investigation yet it still remains grand challenges for all scientists. To gain an in-depth understanding for the catalytic behavior of multimetallic nanomaterials in electrocatalysis, the precise identification of catalytically active sites is highly necessary because it can reveal the interaction mechanism between metal species. Active sites of electro­ catalysts and their catalytic properties can be experimentally demon­ strated, the direct observation of intermediates in reaction process is also important for indisputable results. Recently, using in situ charac­ terization approaches is helpful for probing catalyst states and important reaction intermediates on electrochemical conditions. Specifically, vibrational spectroscopic technologies, including Raman and Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR)-based methods, are proved to be effective tools to identify active sites, surface state changes, and inter­ facial intermediates under operando conditions [200]. Noteworthy, Raman or FTIR alone is unable to provide a comprehensive information of a complex catalytic system. Raman is better than FTIR in probing bond vibrations in low frequency range without water interference. While FTIR has an edge over in confirming the role of interfacial water and adsorbed electrolytes.
Furthermore, other in situ characterization approaches, such as in situ X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and in situ X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS), differential electrochemical mass spectrometry (DEMS), and M¨ossbauer spectroscopy, also can be employed to attain wide insights into the surface evolution and interfacial reaction occur­ ring in electrocatalytic process [72,201,202]. For example, G¨orlin et al. reported a kinetic study of Ni–Fe LDH on OER conditions [201]. The combined DEMS and XAS analyses verified that the addition of Fe could improve the kinetics of the metal-reduction step, leading to an enhancement of the OER activity. In order to obtain the comprehensive information related to the evolution of the structure, composition and surface state changes of $\mathrm{Co_{2}F e O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}}$ NPs, many character­ ization methods including XAS, XPS, atom probe tomography (APT) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) were uti­ lized during cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements under OER condi­ tions [203]. Using in situ attenuated total reflection-based FTIR (ATRFTIR) spectroscopy, Nayak et al. determined the reaction intermediates of ORR on nanostructured Pt electrodes [204]. Based on XAS analysis, Wang et al. concluded that the NiOOH phase instead of Ni–Co spinel itself served as the main redox sites because only Ni displayed redox behavior for OER process [205]. To gain more direct evidence for un­ derstanding the real structure of multi-metal nanomaterials as well as their catalytic mechanisms, more advanced instruments and techniques are highly needed.
## 5. Summary and outlook
The secondary-metal incorporation is confirmed to provide new geometrically active sites for catalysts or manipulate the inherent elec­ tronic and/or surface structures by electron transfer between host and guest metals toward a powerful synergetic effect for higher catalytic activity [206,207]. Because of the different electron-donating abilities of metals, the changes in electronic structure are different, in turn affecting the catalytic properties. Besides, the incorporation of foreign metals can offer additional active sites by activating inert atomic sites, leading to an enhanced catalysis [208]. Based on the adsorption energy calculations, screening of promising metal combinations has been conducted by selecting appropriate dopants. Herein, the superiorities of MOF for construction of multi-metal nanomaterials are summarized. Impor­ tantly, six primary design strategies for multi-metallic nanomaterials from MOFs are emphasized. It should be remarked that although these strategies are separately discussed, two or more pathways are adopted simultaneously in some cases. The combination of different synthetic approaches can afford vast opportunities for developing other novel polymetallic materials. Then, the recent advances of MOF-derived multimetal nanomaterials for electrocatalytic applications (mainly focus on ORR, OER and HER) is comprehensively discussed. These examples reported till now are truly inspiring, and the nanostructured materials with multi-metallic components using MOFs as precursors/templates have an extremely bright future.
Despite the achieved success in the transformation of polymetallic MOFs to their corresponding derivatives, this research field is still at a burgeoning stage, and more endeavor is required to realize the precise synthesis and large-scale production. Several challenges and personal perspective related to future development are critically proposed as follows:
1) The transformation mechanism from MOFs to their derivatives should be deeply explored. As we know, the particle size, shape and stoichiometry greatly affect the activity, selectively and stability of catalysts; however, these parameters are difficult to rationally con­ trol during the synthetic process. Thus, more investigations about precise synthesis are highly desirable. Meanwhile, the delicate modulation of geometric structures can be achieved through precise synthesis.
2) The construction of MOF derivatives with multiple metals should be considered. To date, many researchers tend to fabricate bimetallic materials. More efforts should be devoted to introducing three or more metals into a MOF skeleton while retain the integrity of structure. Multi-metal ions with different oxidation states can pro­ vide more catalytic sites for the electrochemical reaction, leading to improved intrinsic activity. In addition, the function of metal nodes in catalysis has been widely studied, but the researches on the role of organic ligands are scarce. Therefore, much attention is required to verify the contribution of each component in multi-metallic materials.
3) Different morphologies and structures for polymetallic nano­ materials should be developed. Currently, examples presented mainly focus on the simple hollow and core-shell structures. It is urgent to design new shapes of MOF-derived multi-metallic nano­ materials with abundant active sites, such as cube-, polyhedron-, sheet-, cylinder-, chestnut- or seaurchin-like ones, enabling them to adapt more challenging reactions under harsh conditions. Mean­ while, the morphology of most materials used for catalysis is nano­ particles, which is easy to self-aggregate, leading to a reduction of the number of active sites. Therefore, more complex nanostructures are eager to enrich the warehouse of MOF-derived multi-metallic nanomaterials, such as 0D quantum dots, 2D nanosheets and single atoms.
4) Advanced characterizations combined with the auxiliary computa­ tional calculations are highly recommended. At present, many studies simply attributed the enhanced activity to the synergistic effect; whereas, the real origin of such an effect is still indistinct. To improve the utilization efficiency of these functional materials, it is of critical importance to investigate the specific active centers, the location of second metal and the interactions between different metal components. Therefore, using some advanced technologies such as X-ray absorption spectroscopy comprising both XANES and EXAFS, aberration-corrected HAADF-STEM, and DFT calculations to reveal the real-time structural evolution and better design experi­ ments is necessary, thus providing theoretical guidance for the preparation of multi-metallic nanomaterials from MOFs with high performance.
5) Novel synthetic protocols for well-designed nanomaterials are imperatively needed. The synthesis of polymetallic MOF derivatives usually involves several calcination steps at high temperature, which is averse to preserve the periodic pore structure from parent MOFs. Moreover, the generated toxic tail gas during pyrolysis might in­ crease the environmental risks. As the practical applications are the ultimate goal, the optimization on synthetic procedure is eager to achieve the mass production and low cost for industrial applications.
In conclusion, the large diversity of MOF structures and compositions makes them as suitable platform for the construction of polymetallic nanomaterials with desirable properties. The rapid development of these functional materials will bring excellent opportunities in the fields such as adsorption, catalysis, energy conversion and storage and so on, much beyond that of monometallic materials derived from MOFs. In the near future, numerous efforts should be devoted to this exciting area to realize the large-scale engineering and commercial applications.
